Goal: Demonstrate the dangers of poor cybersecurity through a honeypot deployment, show how to collect/analyze attack logs, and present mitigations.

1. Project Objectives

Explain what a honeypot is and why attackers target weak systems.

Demonstrate attack attempts in a controlled environment.

Analyze captured logs for patterns and insights.

Present concrete defense recommendations.

2. System Architecture

Victim Environment: Simulated vulnerable system (isolated VM or container).

Honeypot: Low/medium-interaction honeypot emulating common services (SSH/web).

Log Collection: Centralized log aggregation (ELK, Graylog, or simple CSVs).

Analysis/Visualization: Charts showing attack trends, top IPs, usernames, and commands.

Mitigation: Show improved security (patching, firewalls, MFA, segmentation).

3. Team Roles (3 Members)

Member A – Deployment: Build and configure honeypot(s), manage isolation.

Member B – Analysis: Aggregate logs, create visuals, summarize data.

Member C – Presentation: Explain background, findings, and mitigations.

4. Deliverables

7–10 minute presentation with slides.

Short demo or recorded attack clip (30–90 seconds).

One-page findings + mitigation summary.

Optional sanitized logs.

5. Slide Outline (Example 9-Min Plan)

Title & Team (20s)

Problem & Motivation (45s) – Why honeypots matter.

Architecture Diagram (45s) – Safe lab setup.

Demo (90s) – Short honeypot clip.

Log Analysis (90s) – Show data + insights.

What Went Wrong (45s) – Exposed vulnerabilities.

Mitigations (60s) – Security best practices.

Lessons Learned (40s) – Key takeaways.

Wrap-Up & Q&A (25s)

6. Demo Safety

Run only in an isolated lab (no public exposure).

Use low-interaction honeypots only.

Record demo to avoid live unpredictability.

Anonymize logs (replace IPs/usernames).

Never execute captured malware.

7. Data to Collect & Analyze

Connection metadata: timestamp, source IP/port, destination port.

Authentication attempts: usernames/passwords tried.

Commands entered (if recorded).

Frequency/time trends, top IPs, and port usage.

Visualization ideas: bar chart (usernames), line chart (attack timeline), table (top IPs), pie chart (ports).

8. Tools

Data: CSV/JSON or ELK/Graylog.

Visualization: Excel, Python (pandas + matplotlib), or Kibana.

Presentation: PowerPoint, Google Slides, or Canva.

9. Mitigations to Present

Remove default credentials.

Enforce strong passwords and MFA.

Apply regular patches and minimize exposed ports.

Use fail2ban/firewall rules for brute-force protection.

Segment networks and centralize logging.

Conduct regular security audits.

10. Ethics & Safety

Never expose live honeypots to the public internet without approval.

Keep all activity within institutional or lab environments.

Sanitize all logs and avoid sharing raw IPs or credentials.

Follow course/institution cybersecurity policies.

11. Grading Breakdown (Suggested)

Objective clarity – 10%

Safe architecture – 20%

Demo quality – 25%

Analysis/visuals – 20%

Mitigation insight – 15%

Presentation teamwork – 10%
